75
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
The rest of this podcast is taken up with an interview I conducted last week with a fellow named Alex Koseff.

76
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Alex is the author of the Appalachian Mountain Club Guide to Outdoor Leadership.

77
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
I know that some of you have already got this book and you've read it and you've benefited from it.

78
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
If you want a college level course on outdoor leadership, this is the book.

79
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
This is the one that you want to have.

80
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
It's very well written, It has a lot of good information in it,

81
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,000
And in the course of the interview, Alex shares a tip about working with teenagers that I have not heard before

82
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
And I think it's actually fairly revolutionary.

83
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
It's probably going to save me a lot of angst.

84
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
You know, listen to the interview and see if you can pick it up.

85
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
I'm going to stop talking about the interview so we can actually listen to it.

86
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:43,000
There will be some resources on the blog post that contains this podcast at scoutmastercgcom,

87
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
As you can get in touch with Alex and some training resources and things like that.

88
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
But hey, I've said enough, Let's get started.

89
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Today I'm speaking with Alex Pasek, the founder and director of the Outdoor Safety Institute in Bozeman, Montana.

90
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
We're speaking to Alex from Bozeman

91
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
And he's also the author of the American Mountain Clubs Guide to Outdoor Leadership,

92
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:37,000
which, if you haven't seen it yet, folks, it's a great resource for anybody leading groups in the outdoors

93
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:46,000
And I think it's one of the things that any scout leader who's doing any kind of an ambitious outdoor trip should have in his library.

94
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,000
How are you doing, Alex?

95
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
I'm doing well. I'm pleased to be able to speak to you today, Clark.

96
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
So how did this whole outdoor thing begin for you?

97
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
I grew up actually on the East Coast in Rhode Island and I was always near the ocean

98
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And the first kind of real outdoor exposure I had was just kind of exploring the coastline of Rhode Island and did a lot of fishing and snorkeling.

99
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:17,000
It also was, you know, my family did a lot of family camping and kind of doing getting outside

100
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And that just sparked a love for it. that started like continued into high school when I started doing outdoor activities with friends

101
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:39,000
And then into college when I, you know, I really started backpacking and doing other activities more in a wilderness setting and then leading trips for my college outdoor club as a volunteer

102
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:46,000
And started working in the summers leading wilderness trips for teenagers.

103
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:56,000
So Rhode Island- you got into the Whites and the mountains of New Hampshire and Maine and things like that. or Massachusetts,

104
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
That was my kind of. my first love were the Whites.

105
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:06,000
In some places I spent a lot of time, including some of that time I was working for the Appalachian Mountain Club

106
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
But spent a lot of time there- the mountains in Maine to New Hampshire.

107
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Where did you go to college? I had it on the resume here, but I

108
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
I actually went to college in Minnesota. I went to McAllister College, which is in St Paul,

109
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:23,000
And somehow landed in a spot that didn't have mountains, which,

110
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
But you went canoeing.

111
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Yes, I did. I did get to do a lot of canoeing and actually I learned to rock climb in Minnesota.

112
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
There's actually some good small rock climbing areas there.

113
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
I mean I don't actually regret it at all. It's a really great college experience.

114
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:43,000
And now you're in like one of the big outdoor mechas of the US out there in Bozeman,

115
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Yeah, I mean I can be skiing, fishing, rock climbing, hunting, backpacking- all within 15 to 20 minutes in my house,

116
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
An hour from Yellowstone. It's a pretty amazing place to be able to live.

117
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
That is amazing.

118
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:05,000
The guide when it first came out- I think the first edition was published around 2003..

119
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:09,000
And I got a copy of it then. It was really, really useful.

120
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
It was around the same time that we started doing some extended canoe trips with our scouts.

121
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,000
It taught me a great deal. The second edition was just released, September of last year.

122
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
What do you think the big differences are between the first and the second edition?

123
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
First off, the second edition just is a little bit more refined and readable.

124
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
There was a lot more effort put into the editing of this one,

125
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
So it's just that much more accessible.

126
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Secondarily, there were some big new pieces added,

127
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:48,000
One of them on teaching and facilitation, a lot more on safety and risk management in the outdoors.

128
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And then the biggest piece that was added and a big focus in this edition

129
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
is on needing youth in the outdoors,

130
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,000
And that's something that the Appalachian Mountain Club, who was the publisher,

131
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,000
as well as myself, are really interested in is kind of reversing the trend

132
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
of declining youth participation in outdoor activities

133
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and making sure kids are exposed to the outdoors.

134
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:20,000
The real foundation of the book is in your definition of good outdoor leadership.

135
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
What can you tell me about that?

136
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
The way I define it is that outdoor leadership has three kind of foundational components.

137
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
In the book I kind of use the metaphor of a three-legged stool

138
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and each leg is important for supporting your overall outdoor leadership ability.

139
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:45,000
But those legs or foundations are technical skills, interpersonal skills and judgment skills.

140
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And technical skills are what you know, that's, your ability to know how to set up tents

141
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and cook food and navigate and all those kind of things.

142
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Interpersonal skills, you know, are the ability to work with other leaders

143
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
and with members of your group in a harmonious way.

144
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And then judgment skills: the ability to see what's going on around you,

145
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:15,000
both in the environment and in your group and even with yourself,

146
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and be able to assess what's going on and make good decisions.

147
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
And you know I certainly focus on safety in the book,

148
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:32,000
but it's also that judgment also relates to producing a good outcome,

149
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,000
a good educational experience or a good overall experience

150
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
for the participants in your group as well.

151
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
The judgment piece- you can learn some things about it through reading,

152
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
but it's mostly an experience-based skill, wouldn't you say?

153
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Absolutely, You know, and that's what I say in the book-

154
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
is that this book is a framework, it's a starting point,

155
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:59,000
but the best leaders are the ones who are getting that hands-on experience

156
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,000
and developing those judgment skills based on experience.

157
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
That said, I do think there's room in there for developing those judgment skills

158
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
through really good training programs.

159
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And some of the Wilderness First Aid type courses and leadership courses out there

160
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
that try and put you in simulations or scenarios

161
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
can really help develop some of those judgment skills.

162
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,000
But at the end of the day, nothing beats experience working with

163
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and leading groups in the outdoors for building up those judgment skills.

164
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
In Scouting we have a pretty good training program

165
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
but it's mostly about scouting.

166
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:42,000
Some of it is about outdoor skills but it's a relatively small percentage of it.

167
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
You mentioned the Wilderness First Responder training

168
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and in addition to that, what are the other opportunities out there

169
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:55,000
for people to get some really good scenario-based outdoor leadership training?

170
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Unfortunately, Clark, there's sort of few and far between.

171
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
I mean, in different parts of the country there's opportunities.

172
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
The Appalachian Mountain Club in the Northeast

173
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
operates a program called Mountain Leadership School

174
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
which is a five-day program that's very scenario-based.

175
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
A well-known one is the National Outdoor Leadership School,

176
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:20,000
which offers some phenomenal courses, primarily in the western US,

177
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and some international stuff.

178
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
Those tend to be longer courses, at least two weeks,

179
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
and often a month or longer.

180
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:32,000
There's also a lot of other smaller regional organizations

181
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
that do offer some good scenario-based training.

182
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Let's talk about this Wilderness First Responder course.

183
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I was able to take that and I thought it was great.

184
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
It was fairly easy.

185
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
I did it over a course of two Saturdays a year or so ago.

186
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
How would you encourage somebody to go and take that course?

187
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
I think that the two-day Wilderness First Aid course

188
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
is just absolutely critical for anyone who's venturing

189
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,000
outside of the reach of the quick response

190
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
from the 911 and EMS network.

191
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:10,000
I've dealt with quite a few backcountry emergencies,

192
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
some of them life-threatening.

193
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
It's so important to be able to fall back

194
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
to that basic framework about what to think about first.

195
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
what are your steps in your process

196
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
of dealing with that situation?

197
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
I encourage people to take that course whenever I can

198
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,000
You go on in the new edition of the book

199
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
and elaborate on some techniques

200
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
that can be used with teenagers?

201
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
Most of our scouts are going to be participating

202
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
in these kinds of activities,

203
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
or at least 13 and up to 17, 18 years old.

204
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
What's new out there?

205
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Honestly, Clark, I don't know that any of it's totally new.

206
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Some of this is good practices

207
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
that good people, good leaders in the scouts,

208
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
in mountain clubs in Outward Bound

209
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
and other organizations have been doing for decades,

210
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
But what I'm trying to do is put it together

211
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
in a coherent manner and make it a neat package

212
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
for people in some ways.

213
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:14,000
So the thing that is really important to me

214
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,000
is that the outdoor experience

215
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,000
be something that's different from teenagers' normal life.

216
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:28,000
If you go out there and you you know,

217
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
it's kind of a situation that they experience

218
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
much like they're at home, where teachers or parents

219
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
are always telling them what to do.

220
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
it's not going to be nearly as valuable

221
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:43,000
as if you give the teenagers some progressive responsibility

222
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:48,000
to take on ownership of the activity,

223
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And I think that's something that's really missing

224
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
for a lot of youth today,

225
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:58,000
And so what I'm suggesting is that progressively

226
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
turn over responsibility for the trip

227
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:05,000
so that teens are really both responsible for things,

228
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
which they aren't always.

229
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
So they have to pack their own canoe, cook their own food,

230
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and if they don't do a good job, there are consequences.

231
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
They take over more responsibility

232
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
And eventually, by the end of a- you know, a week-long trip,

233
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:25,000
they may be doing all the navigation and you as a leader

234
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
may be sort of watching from a distance

235
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
to make sure that nothing goes catastrophically wrong,

236
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:36,000
but giving them a little bit of a leeway

237
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
to make some mistakes that aren't going to be,

238
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:42,000
you know, have any real harm or safety repercussions,

239
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And I think that that experience is something

240
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:50,000
that is really valuable in terms of building interest

241
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
in the outdoors but also building, you know,

242
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
overall life skills, decision-making skills

243
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
for the teenagers

244
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
And, ultimately, the activities we're doing in the outdoors.

245
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
you know there's some safety issues

246
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
There's, you know there's some skill involved,

247
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
but they aren't that complicated.

248
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
They're a fairly discreet set of skills

249
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
and you can train people in those pretty quickly,

250
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
especially if they're not, you know

251
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
we're not talking about, you know,

252
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
climbing glaciated mountains, but you know

253
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
the kind of basic activities we do in the outdoors.

254
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
The other thing I think is important is kind of finding

255
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
the right balance of challenge and reward.

256
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
And I've been with groups and I've seen other groups

257
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
out there on the trail where they just kind of

258
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
on these huge marches

259
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
And that can be rewarding if everyone's bought

260
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:45,000
into the idea of accomplishing a lot of miles

261
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and getting something done.

262
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,000
But sometimes, you know, giving the group a chance

263
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
to climb a peak with an amazing view

264
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
and having a real feeling of success that way

265
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
may be more important than pushing them

266
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
really, really hard or having the opportunity

267
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
to have a campfire at night, you know,

268
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
after a solid day of paddling

269
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
is more important than getting in your 30 mile

270
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
paddle that day.

271
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
So there's this fine balance of figuring out

272
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
what's the appropriate amount of challenge for a group

273
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
and how can the activities that you're doing

274
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
be rewarding, either through a sense of accomplishment

275
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
or also some kind of other you know,

276
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
a view or a, you know, a fun night

277
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
on the campfire or something like that.

278
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:35,000
So it's not all about covering miles,

279
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:38,000
No, I mean, I think that some of the groups that

280
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
you know have gotten the most

281
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
that I've led, that have gotten the most out of it,

282
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
have had their challenging days

283
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
and they've had to push a bit, but they've also had

284
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
easier days where there were a lot of fun things:

285
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
swimming a lake- you know fun things

286
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:55,000
to do out there too- and a chance to bond

287
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,000
with teenagers in a way, other teens

288
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
in a way that they don't always get to do

289
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
at home.

290
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
There's a bit of advice that you give

291
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
that you may be unaware of that. it's pretty

292
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
unique, but I haven't seen it anywhere else

293
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
other than in your book, and that,

294
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
especially when working with teenagers,

295
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:18,000
is feeding them

296
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
and frequent snacks and things like that.

297
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
I mean that actually never really occurred to me

298
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
And what can you tell me about that?

299
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
I think it's brilliant.

300
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Well, it's something that I mean.

301
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
I've heard this from other people,

302
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
but I've also had this experience:

303
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
The group morale and motivation

304
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and energy just sort of

305
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
precipitously dropping and all of a sudden

306
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,000
kids who were really excited to be there

307
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
they're not anymore

308
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
And it took me a while to put it together.

309
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,000
but often some food will

310
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
really really help to solve that situation.

311
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
And the science behind that is

312
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:04,000
teenagers have really really high metabolism levels

313
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,000
and they need to eat a lot more frequently

314
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:10,000
than most adults do to keep up their blood sugar levels.

315
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
And when those blood sugar levels drop,

316
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,000
attitudes really go south

317
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
And it has a huge impact

318
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
on the ability of the group

319
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,000
to work together and get things done.

320
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,000
The key there is that if you're at all hungry,

321
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
your group is almost certainly-

322
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:33,000
almost certainly- hungrier than you are

323
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:36,000
And maybe not even be that tuned into that.

324
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:39,000
but making sure people have the opportunity to say,

325
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
hey, I need a snack,

326
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
And even encouraging that,

327
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
before people bring it up,

328
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:48,000
is a great way to keep the group moving.

329
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:51,000
Just feed and hydrate your group.

330
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:54,000
It's not just the food, the water helps a lot too.

331
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,000
It's one of the most basic but helpful tips

332
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,000
I've ever come across.

333
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:03,000
I got to tell you, I know this.

334
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:06,000
I think I knew this at some level,

335
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:09,000
but it was never like a part of the plan

336
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:12,000
And that is worth the price of admission

337
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:15,000
on the book alone.

338
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:18,000
Well, it's just hugely important

339
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,000
And you're right, all of us who've done any work

340
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:24,000
with scouts have encountered this

341
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
in one way or another.

342
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,000
All of a sudden, everybody just kind of hits the wall.

343
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
They start dropping like flies.

344
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
You're wondering what the heck is going on.

345
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
It's just a granola bar away, really,

346
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
Exactly.

347
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
Another piece of that, too,

348
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
is teenagers, especially males,

349
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
have incredibly high appetites.

350
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:51,000
They can really really eat a lot of food,

351
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:54,000
And most American boys

352
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
are used to getting all the food that they want.

353
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,000
And, of course, if you're backpacking,

354
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
you have to carry that food.

355
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
But my advice in general is,

356
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
especially when you're packing for teenage boys,

357
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
is: pack on the heavier side.

358
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:14,000
Carry a little bit more than to have

359
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,000
kids who are complaining about being hungry

360
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,000
or potentially really are hungry

361
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and don't have the food to keep themselves well fueled,

362
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:26,000
And they don't normally associate hunger

363
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:29,000
with a drop in morale or a drop in behavior.

364
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
They just get cranky.

365
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
Exactly,

366
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
They're not even aware:

367
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
wow, I'm hungry.

368
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
That makes me cranky,

369
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
that's the fact of what I'm doing.

370
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
They just go there

371
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
And so being able to notice that

372
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
is pretty golden.

373
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
That's excellent.

374
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:53,000
One of the things that I really found useful

375
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,000
when I got the first copy of this book-

376
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
and it's in the second edition

377
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
and it's a little expanded-

378
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
is your discussion on expedition behavior.

379
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
Why is expedition behavior different

380
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
from evening meetings that we have

381
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and things like that?

382
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
Because expedition behavior is that

383
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:13,000
you're really interdependent upon each other

384
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and the group isn't going to function well

385
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
if people aren't working together

386
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
in somewhat of a harmonious way.

387
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
It really comes out of kind of expeditionary

388
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,000
climbing, like climbing big heat peaks

389
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
in the Himalayas or in Alaska.

390
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
but the concepts are very applicable

391
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
to any outdoor kind of experience,

392
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
especially multi-day ones.

393
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
but it sort of begins with

394
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
the idea of self-awareness,

395
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
so being aware of what your own needs are,

396
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
and then it flows into the idea

397
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
of taking responsibility

398
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
for taking care of your own needs.

399
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,000
A classic example is somebody

400
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
goes off to dinner

401
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
and leaves their sleeping bag out

402
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
and it starts to rain

403
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
and then their sleeping bag is wet

404
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
And that has an impact on the entire group

405
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
because they didn't take

406
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
on their own kind of self-leadership

407
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:11,000
and taking care of their own things.

408
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
And then it moves on from there

409
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,000
to the idea of being kind of supportive

410
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
of group goals,

411
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
so committed to group goals,

412
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
tolerant of each other.

413
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
I'm sure you've had this experience.

414
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
People come into these things

415
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
with different comfort levels

416
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
for being in the outdoors

417
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
and different physical ability levels

418
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
And it's often easy

419
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
for those who are more comfortable

420
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
or physically able to be,

421
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
let's see, like critical

422
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
or not supportive of those

423
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
who are less comfortable.

424
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
but if people are supportive

425
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
within the group and committed

426
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
to the group goals,

427
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
that support can really really help

428
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
those people who are struggling through,

429
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
can make them feel more

430
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
part of the group

431
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
and it can help them

432
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
to really overcome some of those challenges

433
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
and make the experience a success.

434
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
And another piece

435
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
of that whole expedition behavior

436
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
is good communication

437
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and being able

438
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
to express yourself in a

439
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
supportive, non-critical way

440
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
with other members in the group.

441
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
So the expedition

442
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
behavior concept is something that

443
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
leaders really need to

444
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
have in themselves,

445
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
but it's something that the whole group

446
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
can really

447
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
commit to. and one of the things

448
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
that I like to do with groups

449
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
of teenagers particularly

450
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
is introduce this kind of concept

451
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
of expedition behavior- or you can

452
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
call it something else, if you want-

453
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
early on and have the group

454
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
help to define what the

455
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
expectations of each other are.

456
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
And you might have to prompt them a little.

457
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
Do you say, do you want people to be

458
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
supportive when you're having a hard time

459
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
or do you want them to be critical?

460
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
They're inevitably, especially

461
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
within the scouting community, they're going to understand

462
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
the value of being

463
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
supportive and

464
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
that early commitment from the group

465
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
to those concepts can be really

466
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
helpful later on, when all you have

467
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
to do is kind of say: hey, remember,

468
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
early in the

469
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
trip we talked about expedition

470
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
behavior and being supportive

471
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
of one another when we're having a hard time.

472
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
and for a lot of teens

473
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
that'll really click and they say: oh yeah,

474
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
you know, Joe's having a hard

475
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
time. now's not the time

476
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
to complain about

477
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
him slowing us down. now's the time

478
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
to maybe take a little weight from him

479
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
and joke with him a little and try

480
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
and share. You hit on something else

481
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
that I think in their limited

482
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
awareness, teenagers don't always

483
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
realize If they're

484
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
uncomfortable or if they're physically incapable

485
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
of doing something, they

486
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
get kind of afraid and they get very reluctant

487
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
and they get really recalcitrant-

488
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
I think that's the word I'm looking for-

489
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
about moving ahead

490
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
or trying to do anything. and I don't

491
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
think that they associate- just like they don't

492
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
associate being cranky

493
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
or difficult

494
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
with not having enough to eat

495
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
or drink- they don't associate

496
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
the fact that they're kind of afraid

497
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
or uncomfortable with

498
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
something, with the way that they

499
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
behave.

500
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
So, understanding

501
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
that and kind of

502
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
inculcating in the group

503
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
these expedition behaviors that you're talking

504
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
about,

505
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
you're going to avoid an awful lot of that

506
